From: Flax BMC <bmc@flaxadvisors.com>
Subject: [PATCH] power-control: reset OperatingSystemState to Inactive on host power-on

On TiogaPass the POST_COMPLETE GPIO is pulled high and stays asserted
even when the host is powered off.  x86-power-control reads this GPIO
at startup and initialises OperatingSystemState to "Standby" (POST
complete) even with the host off.  The ipmid allowlist filter reads
that state at startup, sets postCompleted=true, and keeps the KCS
channel in restricted mode for the rest of the session.

When the host later powers on the PostComplete GPIO never de-asserts
(it was already high), so postCompleteHandler() never fires "Inactive"
and the filter stays restricted throughout BIOS POST.  BIOS probe
commands arrive on the KCS channel and are rejected with
ccInsufficientPrivilege, causing the BIOS to abort its SMBIOS MDR V2
push.  /var/lib/smbios/smbios2 is never written, and Redfish shows
no CPU/DIMM inventory.

Fix: when HostState transitions to Running (host DC power on), explicitly
set OperatingSystemState to Inactive before PostComplete can assert.
postCompleteHandler() will set it back to Standby when the BIOS
actually asserts the PostComplete GPIO near the end of POST.

This mirrors the existing fix at power-off (the upstream comment about
POST_COMPLETE GPIO not working on some platforms).

---
 src/power_control.cpp | 7 +++++++
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+)

--- a/src/power_control.cpp
+++ b/src/power_control.cpp
@@ -1701,6 +1701,13 @@ static void setOperatingSystemState(const OperatingSystemStateStage stage)
             if (*currentHostState ==
                 "xyz.openbmc_project.State.Host.HostState.Running")
             {
+                // POST_COMPLETE GPIO may be stuck asserted on this platform
+                // when host powers on (hardware pullup keeps it high when
+                // off).  Reset to Inactive now so the IPMI allowlist filter
+                // passes BIOS KCS commands during POST.
+                // postCompleteHandler() will update back to Standby when the
+                // BIOS actually drives PostComplete at the end of POST.
+                setOperatingSystemState(OperatingSystemStateStage::Inactive);
                 pohCounterTimerStart();
                 // Clear the restart cause set for the next restart
                 clearRestartCause();
